(* Width: how many columns of a terminal a character takes, so that a
   program with a screen can wrap its lines and cut them at the
   window's edge.

   A character is 0 columns wide if it is a combining mark (Unicode's
   general categories Mn and Me), a format character (Cf) other than
   the soft hyphen and the marks set over the digits after them
   (Prepended_Concatenation_Mark), a control character (Cc), whose
   effect is the program's to decide, one of Hangul's medial and final
   jamo, which join the syllable before them (1160H to 11FFH, 0D7B0H to
   0D7FFH), or a Hangul filler (3164H, 0FFA0H). It is 2 if Unicode's
   East Asian Width is Wide or Fullwidth, as the ideographs, the kana
   and most emoji are. Every other one, the ambiguous ones among them,
   is 1, as most terminals take them. These are the answers of glibc's
   wcwidth, but for a control, which is -1 there, and a few format
   characters glibc counts as 1.

   It knows characters, not clusters: an emoji joined to another by a
   zero width joiner, or a flag, two regional indicators, counts as its
   parts. The tables below are made from Unicode's data by
   tools/Widths.raz, which says how; they take about 4 KB, which a
   program that doesn't call width or columns doesn't carry. Its test
   uses Check. *)
module Width;
import Str, Check;

(* The tables: each a sorted list of ranges, first and last. Made by
   tools/Widths.raz: don't edit them, make them again. *)
(* tables *)
